Addendum 2026-07-23 — state update at 1.5.1 + measured Tier-2
performance evidence and targets
-------------------------------------------------------------

State delta since the 1.3.0 draft (part of Tier 1 has landed):

  - CLAL-OPTIMIZATION / CLAL-OPTIMIZE / CLAL-COMPILE are now DEFINED
    and registered (1.2.59, debugger-clal-eval branch):
    autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp:8408-8410, implementation
    :4769-4880. CLAL-COMPILE builds an interpreted usubr and weaves
    the instrumented fork under DEBUG>0; SPEED remains pinned at 0
    (api.lisp:4835 "pinned until Tier 2").
  - Still absent: CLAL-COMPILE-FILE, CLAL-COMPILED-FILE-OPTIMIZATION,
    CLAL-FUNCTION-OPTIMIZATION, the fasl format, and VLISP-COMPILE is
    still the nil stub.

Performance evidence (why Tier 2, quantified). The autolisp-benchmark
suite (1.0.0) run on clautolisp 1.5.1 and BricsCAD V26 on the SAME
macOS machine (benchmark-results.org, 2026-07-22) puts the interpreter
behind LispEx exactly where per-form dispatch dominates, and ahead
where the runtime library dominates (us/iteration):

  class          clautolisp  BricsCAD  ratio
  arithmetic        7.23       0.53    13.7x behind
  lists             6.37       0.87     7.4x behind
  strings           9.14       5.39     1.7x behind
  file-io          177.9      114.6     1.6x behind
  serialization    23.17      41.40     1.8x AHEAD
  entity           12.79      23.69     1.9x AHEAD

Ceiling measurement: hand-transpiling the two losing workloads to CL
exactly as Tier-2 codegen would emit them, compiled under SBCL on the
same machine (companion file compiler-transpile-probe.lisp — runnable,
results recorded in its header):

  workload    interpreter  BricsCAD  conservative(A)  optimising(B)
  arithmetic     7.23        0.53        0.11-0.14      0.017-0.024
  lists          6.37        0.87        0.12-0.15      0.12-0.14

Strategy consequences for Tier-2 codegen (the tuning):

  1. Conservative-first is enough for parity and probably the win.
     Variant A keeps every AutoLISP local dynamically scoped (CL
     specials) and all arithmetic generic/boxed, and still beats
     LispEx 4-6x. Dynamic scoping is NOT the bottleneck — SBCL
     special access is cheap. Escape analysis / lexical promotion /
     type declamation (Variant B: another 6x on arithmetic, nil on
     lists) is a later optimisation pass, not a prerequisite.
  2. The margin to protect is BUILTIN CALL OVERHEAD. The probe maps
     AutoLISP ops onto raw CL ops; codegen that instead full-calls the
     closed runtime builtins (argument list consing + checks per op)
     erodes the 4-6x margin by an estimated 3-5x, i.e. down to bare
     parity. Tier 2 must therefore open-code the hot core operators
     as inline CL carrying AutoLISP semantics — at least:
     + - * / 1+ 1- < <= > >= = /= car cdr cons list setq-refs, and
     the list walkers (nth member assoc length reverse append) —
     falling back to the runtime builtins for the long tail.
  3. Where the compiler will NOT move the needle (set expectations in
     the acceptance): file-io (syscall/buffer-bound), memory-gc
     (SBCL's collector, not dispatch), serialization/entity (already
     ahead; library-bound).

Tier-2 quantitative acceptance (extends the qualitative sketch above;
instrument = autolisp-benchmark suite 1.0.0 — numbers are only
comparable at the same suite version — via `make -C autolisp-benchmark
run` vs `run-bricscad-macos` on one machine; the CAD run needs
alfe-request-timeout-aborts-long-eval fixed first):

  - arithmetic and lists at or below BricsCAD V26 us/iter on the same
    hardware (floor: parity; target: 2x+ ahead, i.e. <= ~0.4 us/iter
    arithmetic, <= ~0.5 us/iter lists on the reference machine).
  - strings within 1.2x of BricsCAD (from 1.7x behind).
  - no regression on serialization / entity / file-io vs the
    interpreter baseline in benchmark-results.org.
  - benchmark suite version bumped if any workload changes; record
    before/after tables in benchmark-results.org.


================================================================
IMPLEMENTATION LOG (Claude, branch implement-compiler, 2.0.x)
================================================================

Slice 1 -- 2.0.0/2.0.1: the transpiler skeleton
-----------------------------------------------
clautolisp/autolisp-compiler, one design decision: anything the
transpiler cannot translate compiles to (autolisp-eval '<form> context).
The compiler is therefore CORRECT FROM ITS FIRST LINE and gets faster as
coverage grows, instead of being wrong until complete. Constants, symbol
references, QUOTE, PROGN, IF, SETQ, AND, OR, COND, WHILE open-coded;
everything else fell back. Equivalence corpus: compiled and interpreted
evaluation must AGREE (not "the compiler handles these forms").

Slice 2 -- 2.0.2: function calls
--------------------------------
A call compiles to CALL-AUTOLISP-FUNCTION-IN-CONTEXT with the callee
resolved by RESOLVE-AUTOLISP-FUNCTION-DESIGNATOR. Using the interpreter's
OWN entry point is the point: TRACE, the :subr/:usubr call-stack frames,
the debugger's two-bodies dispatch and the subr host-error wrapping keep
working with no second implementation of any of them.

Dropped deliberately: the per-form (:eval . form) frame AUTOLISP-EVAL
pushes. That cons per evaluated form IS the cost being removed, so
backtraces through compiled code show function frames but not
intermediate form frames. Restoring them is the INSTRUMENTED variant's
job (pjb asked for both), and this is the seam where the two will differ.

The call branch sits behind the runtime's KNOWN-SPECIAL-OPERATOR-P --
the same question the debugger's instrumenter asks -- because a special
operator has unevaluated operands and compiling (defun f (x) ...) as a
call would evaluate its lambda list and body as arguments.

MEASURED: 3.4x-4.2x on fully-compiled loops; 2.3x when the callee is a
user function whose body still runs interpreted.

Bug found and fixed while writing it:
transpiler-t-is-not-self-evaluating (issues/closed/).

Slice 3 -- 2.0.3: compiled function BODIES
------------------------------------------
An AUTOLISP-USUBR now carries a COMPILED-BODY beside its plain and
instrumented ones -- a THIRD fork -- and CALL-AUTOLISP-FUNCTION-IN-CONTEXT
runs it inside the frame BIND-USUBR-FRAME already pushes. Compiling the
body changes how the body is evaluated, not what it is evaluated in.

Weaving is the compiler's, installed through *COMPILE-USUBR-HOOK*: the
same dependency inversion as *INSTRUMENT-USUBR-HOOK*, so an image built
without the compiler behaves exactly as it did before it existed.

LAZY, AT A THRESHOLD (*AUTOLISP-COMPILATION-THRESHOLD*, default 16).
Weaving runs the host CL compiler, which costs far more than
interpreting a short body once, and most functions in a freshly loaded
file are called once or never. A failed compilation stores :FAILED, not
NIL, so it is attempted once and never retried -- NIL means "not tried".

An instrumented body WINS under a debug session: the one place the two
forks compete, and debuggability wins it.

MEASURED (same machine, fresh contexts, values compared):
  fib 21, recursive .............................. 3.00x
  a loop inside one function body ................ 3.37x
  a loop calling a small function 50000 times .... 1.14x   <-- the finding

THAT 1.14x IS THE MOST USEFUL NUMBER IN THIS LOG. A call-dominated
workload barely moves, because what dominates is no longer body
evaluation but the CALL PROTOCOL itself: the trace check, the call-stack
cons, BIND-USUBR-FRAME, the unwind-protect and POP-DYNAMIC-FRAME, none of
which compiling a body touches. Open-coding more operators would not
help that workload at all. So the next slice is the call protocol -- and
this is exactly the "measure, then open-code" order the transpiler's
header argues for: without the measurement the obvious next move
(open-coding + - * / car cdr) would have been the wrong one.

VERIFICATION -- and this matters more than the corpus. `make
test-compiler-equivalence` runs the ENTIRE project corpus (31 suites)
with the threshold at 1, so every AutoLISP function the tests exercise
runs COMPILED, and every existing assertion must still hold. Result: 83
function bodies compiled, zero failures. The script COUNTS the bodies it
compiled and exits non-zero if the count is zero, because a green run
proves nothing if nothing was compiled -- the same failure mode as a
test file that loads without running, which is the worst kind since it
presents as a pass.

Slice 4 -- 2.0.4: the call protocol
-----------------------------------
Slice 3's 1.14x on a call-dominated workload said the bottleneck had
moved out of body evaluation and into the CALL PROTOCOL. Profiled it
(sb-sprof, a tiny function called 2,000,000 times) rather than guessing,
and the profile named three costs that were pure repeated work:

  1. SPLIT-USUBR-LAMBDA-LIST ran on EVERY call -- two POSITION-IFs, up to
     three SUBSEQs and the whole validation -- to re-derive an answer
     fixed when the function object was made. 7% of the profile as
     POSITION-IF. Now memoised on the usubr (USUBR-LAMBDA-LIST-SPLIT).
     Only SUCCESSFUL splits are remembered: a malformed lambda list must
     keep signalling on every call, and computing it eagerly in the
     constructor would move the diagnostic from the call to the DEFUN.

  2. AUTOLISP-FUNCTION-TRACE-P hashed a name STRING in an EQUAL table on
     every call, to discover that nothing is traced. The empty-table test
     now comes first: HASH-TABLE-COUNT is a slot read, and an empty table
     can only answer NIL. This is where GETHASH/EQUAL, SXHASH-STRING and
     STRING= in the profile came from.

  3. A DYNAMIC-FRAME allocated a HASH TABLE in its initform -- one per
     function call, to hold typically a single parameter. Bindings are
     now an alist, promoted to a hash table past
     *DYNAMIC-FRAME-ALIST-LIMIT* (12) so a function with a hundred locals
     does not degrade lookup along the frame chain. Six call sites read
     the slot directly with GETHASH; they now go through FRAME-BINDING /
     (SETF FRAME-BINDING) / FRAME-BOUND-SYMBOLS, so the representation is
     decided in ONE place -- six identical GETHASHes is how two
     representations start disagreeing.

After: POSITION-IF, GETHASH/EQUAL, the string hashing, %MAKE-HASH-TABLE,
%ALLOC-HASH-TABLE, GROW-HASH-TABLE and MAKE-DYNAMIC-FRAME are all gone
from the top of the profile.

MEASURED A/B (min of 5 runs each, the same tree with only these three
changes reverted for the baseline -- NOT inferred from profiler sample
counts, which I first misread off truncated output):

  fib 21, recursive ......... compiled 0.036s -> 0.028s  (~22% faster)
  loop calling a function ... compiled 0.456s -> 0.440s  (~4%)
  loop inside a body ........ compiled 0.156s -> 0.148s  (~5%)

The gain lands on RECURSION and on call-heavy code, which is what it
should: it is per-call overhead that was removed. It benefits the
INTERPRETER equally -- these are shared costs -- so the compiled/
interpreted RATIO barely moves (1.19x on the call-dominated loop). The
ratio is the wrong instrument for this change; absolute time is the
right one.

What still dominates a call-dominated profile:
CALL-AUTOLISP-FUNCTION-IN-CONTEXT itself (17% self) and LOOKUP-FUNCTION
(20% cumulative) -- the function is re-resolved from the symbol on every
call. Caching that at the call site is the obvious next move and the
delicate one, because AutoLISP functions can be redefined at any moment:
the cache would have to key on the binding CELL, not the function value.

Behaviour pinned by four new runtime tests (evaluator-tests.lisp), which
assert what the frame must DO rather than how it stores it: every local
survives the alist-to-table promotion (including the first, bound before
it), locals shadow and restore globals, nested frames shadow
independently, and re-assigning a local replaces rather than accumulates.
They use only special operators, since that suite runs without builtins.

Slice 5 -- 2.0.5: the symbol-to-cell lookup
-------------------------------------------
Slice 4 left LOOKUP-FUNCTION at 20% of a call-dominated profile, most of
it inside NAMESPACE-BINDING-CELL: a TYPECASE on the namespace plus an EQ
hash lookup, run on every variable read, every assignment and every call.

An AUTOLISP-SYMBOL now remembers the last (NAMESPACE . CELL) it resolved
to. A hit is a slot read and an EQ test.

WHY IT IS SOUND, checked rather than assumed: no code path removes an
entry from a namespace's bindings table (no REMHASH anywhere on them),
none clears a table (the only CLRHASH on a binding table is
RESET-AUTOLISP-SYMBOL-TABLE, which discards the symbols themselves and
their caches with them), and no code replaces a table wholesale. So the
symbol-to-cell mapping only ever grows, and a remembered answer stays
right. What a redefinition changes is the cell's VALUE, read through the
cell in both paths -- this caches the LOOKUP, never the DEFINITION.

Two details that are the difference between a cache and a bug:
  - ONE entry, stored as a single CONS. A two-slot cache could be read
    torn -- namespace from one writer, cell from another -- and hand back
    a cell belonging to a different namespace. A cons is built before it
    is published and never mutated.
  - A :CREATEP NIL miss is NOT remembered. Caching `no cell' would make
    the next :CREATEP T call answer NIL from the cache instead of
    creating the cell, leaving the binding permanently unwritable.

MEASURED A/B, min of 5, back to back (the first attempt compared against
numbers taken earlier under different machine load and looked like a
REGRESSION -- it was noise; the back-to-back run settled it):

                             compiled   A -> B     interpreted A -> B
  fib 21, recursive           0.032 -> 0.028      0.096 -> 0.080
  loop calling a function     0.468 -> 0.412      0.548 -> 0.480
  loop inside a body          0.164 -> 0.128      0.572 -> 0.500
  variable-heavy loop         0.556 -> 0.480      0.560 -> 0.488

12-22% across every workload, compiled AND interpreted, which is what a
lookup on the common path of the whole engine should give.

Note the variable-heavy row: compiled and interpreted are within 2% of
each other there. A loop of SETQs and variable reads has almost nothing
for the transpiler to remove -- it emits the same SET-VARIABLE and
LOOKUP-VARIABLE calls the interpreter makes. The transpiler's gain is in
removing DISPATCH, so it shows on control flow and calls, not on variable
traffic. Worth knowing before promising numbers on real drawings' code.

Four tests pin the cache's two silent failure modes: the same symbol in
two namespaces must resolve to two different cells (a wrong namespace
would read another document's variables), alternating namespaces must
merely miss rather than answer wrongly, a :CREATEP NIL miss must not be
remembered, and a redefinition must be visible through the cache.

Slice 6 (2.0.6) -- a symbol remembers whether it is a special operator
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The item at the head of slice 5's STILL OPEN list, done. On every
compound form it evaluates, the interpreter asked "is this operator a
special operator?", and the answer cost a STRING-UPCASE -- which
allocates a fresh string -- followed by an ASSOC with STRING= over the
24-entry dispatch table. For an ordinary function call, the common case,
that scan ran to the end and failed every single time. The answer is now
memoised on the AUTOLISP-SYMBOL itself.

Unlike the binding cache of slice 5, this answer CAN go stale: the table
is runtime-mutable, because the debugger registers its %CLAL-POLL poll
operator into it. So the entry is stamped with
*SPECIAL-OPERATOR-GENERATION*, which REGISTER-SPECIAL-OPERATOR and
UNREGISTER-SPECIAL-OPERATOR both bump; a stale stamp is simply a miss.
Invalidating a whole generation at once costs nothing and never walks
the symbol table.

Two details are the difference between a cache and a bug:

  - A NIL handler is a VALUABLE answer, not a missing one -- it is the
    common case. So the cache entry is a CONS whose CAR is the
    generation: the cons being present is what separates "not a special
    operator" from "not asked yet". Storing the handler alone would have
    disabled the cache for exactly the case it exists to serve.
  - The counter is a DEFVAR, not a DEFPARAMETER, and the file bumps it
    at load time. Reloading api.lisp rebuilds the dispatch table and
    drops anything registered into it at runtime; a counter that reset
    to 0 alongside it would let stale caches match again.

Only a symbol can name a special operator, so a non-symbol operator now
answers NIL without touching the table. Previously a lambda form's
operator went through ASSOC with STRING= and was compared, as a string
designator, against all 24 names -- correct by accident, and 24 string
compares per call.

MEASURED, back to back, minimum of 5 (2.0.5 -> 2.0.6):

  workload                    compiled          interpreted
  fib 24, recursive           0.124 -> 0.120    0.360 -> 0.168
  loop calling a function     0.588 -> 0.276    0.688 -> 0.284
  loop inside a body          0.096 -> 0.092    0.364 -> 0.168
  variable-heavy loop         0.524 -> 0.240    0.516 -> 0.240

The interpreter is 2.1x-2.4x faster on every workload. This is the
largest single win of the six slices, and it is worth understanding why
a dispatch lookup dominated: the forms in a hot AutoLISP loop are tiny,
so a per-form allocation plus a 24-entry string scan was not overhead
around the work, it WAS the work.

Read the compiled column carefully -- it does not say the compiler got
faster. Compiled code never calls SPECIAL-OPERATOR-FUNCTION at run time;
the transpiler resolved that question at compile time. Rows 1 and 3 (a
compiled body) are unchanged, as they must be. Rows 2 and 4 move because
their WHILE loop is top-level code, which is interpreted in both
columns. That the two unchanged rows are exactly the two that should be
unchanged is the check that these numbers mean what they claim.

Five tests pin the invalidation, every failure of which is silent: a
non-operator stays a non-operator across repeated asks, registering an
operator is visible to a symbol that already cached NIL, unregistering
is too, re-registering replaces the handler, a registered operator still
receives its arguments UNEVALUATED end to end, and a lambda-form
operator answers NIL without reaching the table.

Slice 7 (2.0.7) -- the instrumented transpiler variant
------------------------------------------------------

The second of the two variants pjb asked for. They are not two
compilers: they are the same TRANSPILE-FORM given two different bodies.
The non-instrumented variant compiles a function's plain body; the
instrumented one compiles the body the DEBUGGER wove, in which every
instrumentable form is wrapped in a (%CLAL-POLL fid form-id INNER) node.

That node was the entire obstacle. It is a registered special operator,
so the transpiler fell back on it -- and since the OUTERMOST node wraps
the whole body, compiling an instrumented function produced one call to
AUTOLISP-EVAL and gained exactly nothing. Compiling instrumented code
means open-coding that node; there is no other way in.

WHAT IS NOT DUPLICATED. The emitted code is not a reimplementation of
the poll protocol. EVAL-POLL-FORM was refactored into
CALL-WITH-POLL-POINT (fid form-id context THUNK) plus a two-line caller
that supplies (lambda () (autolisp-eval inner context)); compiled code
calls the same function with the transpiled inner form as its thunk. The
shadow stack, the :BEFORE/:AFTER poll points, form-level jumps and the
CLAL-POLL-RETURN restart are written once. A second copy would be a
debugger that steps differently on functions that happen to be hot --
the kind of bug a user would never believe and never reproduce.

LAYERING. The compiler does not depend on the debugger and the debugger
does not depend on the compiler; they meet in the runtime, through
*COMPILED-POLL-HOOK* (debug -> runtime) and
*COMPILE-INSTRUMENTED-USUBR-HOOK* (compiler -> runtime), the same
dependency inversion as *INSTRUMENT-USUBR-HOOK*. +POLL-OPERATOR-NAME+
moved into the runtime for the same reason: three layers speak that
name, and three spellings would be three chances to disagree.

WHAT CHANGED IN THE DISPATCH. The runtime used to ask for a compiled fork
only when *DEBUGGING* was NIL, because the choice was between a
debuggable body and a fast one and debuggability had to win. There is no
longer a choice: the order is now instrumented-and-compiled,
instrumented, compiled, plain.

MEASURED (fib 24 under a live debug session, debug flag on, no
breakpoint hit -- the run-to-breakpoint case; minimum of 5, three runs):

  instrumented, interpreted     0.244 - 0.268
  instrumented, compiled        0.176 - 0.188   =>  1.36x - 1.43x
  no debug session, compiled    0.120 - 0.136

Read against that third row: debugging a hot function used to cost ~2.0x
and now costs ~1.5x. The gain is FAR smaller than the 3x the plain
variant gets, and the reason is worth recording -- the poll protocol
itself (a shadow-stack push, an UNWIND-PROTECT and a RESTART-CASE per
poll point, per form) is now the cost. Compiling can only remove the
interpreter dispatch AROUND that machinery. Making debugging faster from
here means making poll points cheaper, not compiling harder.

HOW IT IS TESTED. `make test-debug-compiled' runs the ENTIRE corpus with
every instrumented body compiled and counts them: 394 compiled, all 31
suites green. That is the real assurance, and it is much stronger than
any corpus of hand-written cases, because what could break here is not a
wrong ANSWER -- it is a poll point firing in a different order, a shadow
stack left unbalanced by a non-local exit, a jump skipping the wrong
form. Those are invisible to an equivalence corpus and are exactly what
the stepping / breakpoint / jump suites already look for.

Six unit tests cover what the corpus cannot, of which one carries the
slice: AN-INSTRUMENTED-BODY-REALLY-COMPILES-RATHER-THAN-FALLING-BACK.
Every other test would pass unchanged if the compiler still emitted one
AUTOLISP-EVAL of the whole woven body -- that behaviour is CORRECT, just
pointless. Verified as a real control: with the %CLAL-POLL branch
disabled, the equivalence cases still all pass and only that one test
fails.

Slice 8 (2.0.8) -- SPEED is unpinned, and the qualities become an algebra
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The compiler had no user-reachable surface. It turned out it had had one
all along: CLAL-OPTIMIZE / CLAL-OPTIMIZATION are specified (two spec
entries, a user-manual section, the fork matrix in the
debugger-public-interface issue), shipped, and SPEED -- "code is
compiled to a faster binary" -- was hard-pinned to 0 with the comment
`pinned until Tier 2'. Tier 2 is slices 1-7. The pin is gone.

WHAT pjb CORRECTED. I had reported SPACE as "reads back but drives
nothing", and treated the matrix as four fixed rows. It is not a table
of rows: it is an algebra over the levels, and SPACE is not a switch of
its own but what DEBUG is WEIGHED AGAINST.

  non-instrumented fork   always present; compiled iff SPEED >= 1
  instrumented fork       present iff DEBUG >= SPACE; compiled iff SPEED >= 2
  eager, at definition    iff SPEED >= 3

That reading gives the intermediate levels something to mean, which four
fixed rows never did. (DEBUG 3) (SPACE 3) says the two matter equally
and KEEPS the instrumented fork -- the >= boundary, and the case most
likely to be got wrong, so it has its own test.

The two forks compiling at different SPEED levels is deliberate and
measured: SPEED 1 is "make my code fast", SPEED 2 is "and while I am
debugging it too", which is a further step because it costs a second
compilation of every function and buys less -- slice 7 measured the poll
protocol, not interpretation, as what makes debugging slow.

SPEED 3 means compile EAGERLY. Sooner is the small part. The point is
that a whole file compiled in ONE with-compilation-unit admits file-wide
optimizations -- literal coalescing, direct function linking instead of
going through the symbol binding -- that compiling one function at a
time, on the call that happened to make it hot, can never do. This slice
implements eager-at-DEFUN; the file-wide unit needs CLAL-COMPILE-FILE
and is the next slice.

THE DEFAULT. ((DEBUG 3) (SPACE 0) (SPEED 2)), not the issue's (SPEED 0).
Taking the 0 literally would have shipped a compiler that is off unless
asked for -- a regression dressed as spec-compliance -- because SPEED 2
is exactly the behaviour the engine already had while the pin was in.
Written up for pjb to revise in speed-default-level.issue; it is one
number in one place.

WHAT WAS NOT TESTED BEFORE. Nothing. There was not a single test for
CLAL-OPTIMIZE or CLAL-OPTIMIZATION anywhere in the tree -- an
AutoLISP-facing builtin, fully specified, with a documented trap in it
(SPEED silently ignored), and no test. Seven now cover the algebra and
the argument errors; four more in the compiler suite cover SPEED end to
end, including that eager compilation of a body the compiler chokes on
must still DEFINE the function and run it interpreted. SPEED 3 must not
become a correctness setting.

Slice 9 (2.0.9) -- CLAL-COMPILE-FILE and CLAL-COMPILE-SYSTEM
-------------------------------------------------------------

"SPEED 3 compiles the whole file at once during LOAD" needed no new
machinery, which is the pleasant part. At SPEED 3 every DEFUN compiles
as it is evaluated (slice 8), so putting LOAD inside one host
compilation unit puts every one of that file's compilations inside it.
CALL-IN-FILE-COMPILATION-UNIT wraps the single point where a file's
forms are evaluated, and the two new builtins are thin on top of it.

ONE CORRECTION TO pjb'S SKETCH, and it matters. The sketch has the
file-level unit at :OVERRIDE T and the system-level one at :OVERRIDE
NIL. Those two cannot both hold: :OVERRIDE T starts a FRESH unit
instead of joining the enclosing one, so with it on the file level
CLAL-COMPILE-SYSTEM's system-wide unit would be inert -- each file it
loaded would open its own and the outer one would contain nothing.
:OVERRIDE NIL on both is what gives the intended semantics: a file's
unit is absorbed into a system's when there is one, and creates its own
when there is not. Implemented that way, with the reason at the call
site so nobody restores the T.

WHAT THE UNIT ACTUALLY BUYS TODAY, stated precisely because the gap
matters. A compilation unit around a run of COMPILE calls defers
undefined-function warnings to its end. That is real and it is exactly
what makes forward references across files work quietly -- the thing pjb
flagged. The wider prize he named (coalescing common literals, linking
calls directly instead of through the symbol binding) belongs to
COMPILE-FILE, not to COMPILE, and arrives with the artefact writer. It
would be easy and wrong to describe this slice as delivering file-wide
optimization.

THE QUALITIES GOVERN, NOT THE NAMES. Below SPEED 3 neither builtin
opens a unit and neither forces compilation; at SPEED 0 they compile
nothing at all and are simply LOAD. That is pjb's specification applied
literally -- "if we had (DEBUG 3) (SPEED 0) (SPACE 0) while calling
clal-compile-system we would have to do just the plain per-file work" --
and it is the right shape: a function called `compile-file' that
compiled regardless of the qualities would be the surprise the qualities
exist to prevent.

OUTPUT-FILE SIGNALS RATHER THAN BEING IGNORED. CLAL-COMPILE-SYSTEM
takes the (output-file source-file-list) API pjb asked for, but nothing
is written to disk yet, so a non-nil OUTPUT-FILE signals
:unsupported-output-file naming the ticket. Of the three ways to handle
an argument the build cannot honour -- ignore it, pretend, refuse --
only refusing avoids sending someone to look for a file that was never
created.

VLISP-COMPILE IS NOT IN THIS SLICE, deliberately. Two reasons. The name
pjb used, VLISP-COMPILE-FILE, does not exist in either vendor: the
specified function is VLISP-COMPILE (mode, source, [output]). And it is
specified to return T when the OUTPUT FILE WAS CREATED. clautolisp
creates none, so the current no-op returning nil is HONEST; making it
return T for an in-memory compilation would claim a file exists.
It becomes real with the artefact, and its 'st / 'lsm / 'lsa modes then
map naturally onto per-file versus system-wide units -- which is what
those mode names mean in the VLISP IDE.

Five tests: it defines what it loads, it honours SPEED rather than its
own name, a missing file returns nil the way LOAD does, a non-nil
OUTPUT-FILE is refused, and a two-file system with a forward reference
from the first file to the second resolves and runs.

Slice 10 (2.0.10) -- the .lap artefact, and VLISP-COMPILE on top of it
----------------------------------------------------------------------

pjb answered the design question of slice 9, and the answer was to stop
trying to be clever. An artefact cannot be a Lisp image: AutoCAD and
BricsCAD load applications INTO a document that already has others
loaded, in any order, so a .lap has to load into a running image. A
native host FASL, renamed, is the only thing that does that.

Cross-host and cross-version portability are therefore GIVEN UP
deliberately, not overlooked. A portable format means a custom byte-code
FASL and bocl's experience with one was not good on speed. Builds that
differ are kept in different directories, as the binary releases already
are; the name carries nothing about the build.

THE PROBLEM WAS NOT THE ONE I HAD WRITTEN DOWN. compiler.issue had said
this needs MAKE-LOAD-FORM. It does not. COMPILE-FILE is the only door to
a FASL, so the artefact is GENERATED AS SOURCE and compiled -- which
makes the problem PRINTING, not externalizing. AUTOLISP-SYMBOL has a
PRINT-OBJECT method that writes its bare name, so printing one into a
generated file yields a token that reads back as a COMMON LISP symbol.
Nothing signals. The artefact is simply wrong, and wrong in a way no
type error would catch.

EXTERNALIZE-DATUM emits CONSTRUCTOR forms instead --
(INTERN-AUTOLISP-SYMBOL "FOO") -- which is what "re-interning by name"
was always for: the symbol lands in the LOADING image's table, the only
table it could usefully be in. The second reason stands as recorded: a
struct read back from #S(...) would be EQ to nothing, and every EQ test
in the runtime would quietly stop matching.

LOAD-TIME-VALUE around every constructed literal, and this one is easy
to miss. Without it a fallback inside a loop -- a plain (AUTOLISP-EVAL
'<form> context) -- rebuilds its form on every iteration. Not merely
slower: source positions are recorded against body conses, so a form
rebuilt per call is a form the debugger can no longer locate.

A .lap KEEPS EACH FUNCTION'S SOURCE BODY beside its compiled one.
Dropping it would make artefacts smaller and every function loaded from
one impossible to instrument, because the debugger weaves its fork FROM
the body.

CLAL-COMPILE-SYSTEM emits all its sources into ONE generated file, so a
single COMPILE-FILE covers the whole system. That is a better answer
than slice 9's WITH-COMPILATION-UNIT: a real single compilation, in
which literals can genuinely be coalesced across the application, rather
than only a shared warning context.

VLISP-COMPILE is now the vendor-API spelling of CLAL-COMPILE-FILE. Its
mode is accepted and ignored as in BricsCAD, and the reason is worth
keeping: 'st versus 'lsm / 'lsa is single-file versus
optimize-and-link-ACROSS-files, and a call naming one source has nothing
to link across -- the cross-file form is CLAL-COMPILE-SYSTEM. An UNKNOWN
mode signals, though: all three real modes do the same thing here, so
ignoring 'st is harmless, but ignoring a misspelling would hide a typo
in a build script indefinitely.

The tests are round trips, not existence checks: compile, then load the
artefact into a FRESH context and run what it defines. A test that only
checked a file appeared would pass for an empty artefact, and for one
whose symbols are interned in a table nobody reads -- which is exactly
the failure the externalizer exists to prevent.

Slice 11 (2.0.12) -- the --optimize option, and the compiler that was
not in the program
-------------------------------------------------------------------

The option itself is small and does exactly what pjb validated: `-O
QUALITY=N,...` / `--optimize=...`, plus `-O0`..`-O3`, setting the
qualities BEFORE the first file is loaded. His reasoning is the whole
justification and is worth keeping verbatim in the code: in CL a DECLAIM
governs the rest of its file because DECLAIM is a macro with a
compilation-time side effect; AutoLISP has neither macros nor a
compilation-time/load-time/run-time distinction, so a (clal-optimize
...) at the top of a .lsp takes effect only when it is EVALUATED -- by
which time the file it was meant to govern has been read and its DEFUNs
built. Nothing inside the language is early enough.

Decisions worth recording:

  The grammar MIRRORS CLAL-OPTIMIZE rather than inventing a second
  vocabulary: QUALITY=N, a bare QUALITY meaning level 3 (as a bare
  quality symbol means there), and a bare N meaning speed=N. One
  concept, two spellings.

  Occurrences ACCUMULATE. `-O speed=3 -O debug=0` is the same request as
  `-O speed=3,debug=0`. Replacing would make the second occurrence
  silently discard the first, which is not what anyone types it for.

  `-O0`..`-O3` are four LITERAL short specs. The shared parser matches
  short options by exact string and has no attached-value rule; the
  -E<situation> family is enumerated the same way. Four specs beat a
  parser change that would affect every option.

  The CLI parses to ((QUALITY . LEVEL) ...) and hands those to
  SET-CLAL-OPTIMIZATION-LEVELS, which goes through
  APPLY-CLAL-OPTIMIZATION exactly as CLAL-OPTIMIZE does. The algebra
  turning levels into mechanism stays in ONE place; a level set from the
  command line cannot come to mean something different from the same
  level set from AutoLISP.

  Applied AFTER BUILD-CONTEXT (INSTALL-CORE-BUILTINS re-derives the
  gates from *CLAL-OPTIMIZATION* and would otherwise undo the request)
  and BEFORE the first action, including the init files.

THE FINDING, which is much bigger than the option:

The shipped clautolisp executable contained NO COMPILER. Ten slices of
it, all tested, none of it in the program. `clautolisp/clautolisp-tool`
did not depend on `clautolisp/autolisp-compiler`, so the compiler was
never loaded, so its (setf *compile-usubr-hook* ...) toplevel forms
never ran, so every hook stayed NIL -- and a NIL hook means the
interpreter runs. By design: that is exactly the configuration in which
the compiler is absent, so nothing warns.

It was found by MEASURING what the new option did, which was nothing:

    before                after
      -O0   851 ms          -O0   916 ms
      -O2   845 ms          -O2   734 ms
      -O3   858 ms          -O3   747 ms
      ratio 1.00            ratio 1.24

(fib 27 through the built SBCL image, best of five. The two columns are
different images; the ratio WITHIN a column is the measurement.)

The dependency inversion that makes the compiler optional is right, and
its consequence is what was missed: because nothing below pulls the
compiler in, the program that ASSEMBLES the components has to name it,
and only the test systems did. Every compiler test system depends on the
compiler directly -- as a test of the compiler must -- so the hooks were
always installed in the test image. The tests were true of the test
image, and were reported as if they were true of the program.

This is the 2.0.11 lesson one level up. There, tests ran on one host and
were reported as holding for both. Here they ran in one image and were
reported as holding for the program. The test was not wrong either time;
the extrapolation was. Recorded as
issues/closed/compiler-absent-from-shipped-binary.issue.

The same commit adds the gate, because fixing the dependency does not
stop it recurring: make test-shipped-compiler builds the executable and
asserts that CLAL-COMPILE-FILE produces an artefact, that the .lap loads
and runs, and that -O reaches (clal-optimization). The probe is
CLAL-COMPILE-FILE rather than a timing comparison because it is the one
surface that distinguishes `no compiler in this build' from every other
failure -- it signals COMPILER-NOT-AVAILABLE instead of falling back.

The gate was verified RED before being believed: dependency removed,
executable rebuilt, three checks fail and the output names the missing
line in clautolisp.asd. A gate never seen red is not a gate -- the same
mistake in miniature as the one it guards against.

Wired into both CI lanes along with test-compiler-equivalence and
test-debug-compiled, which had never been in CI either.

Slice 12 (2.0.13) -- the measurement said something else
--------------------------------------------------------

This slice was going to open-code the hot operators. The standing plan
had named that as where the margin against LispEx is, and it had been
deliberately deferred so that no operator would have a second
implementation able to disagree with the first.

Profiling first said the margin is somewhere else entirely. On compiled
fib 30:

    FRAME-BINDING       18.7% self   31.7% total
    LOOKUP-FUNCTION     17.6% self   52.1% total
    %ASSOC-EQ           14.2% self
    ...
    BUILTIN--            2.4%
    BUILTIN-+            1.1%

Over half the time resolving names; about 4% doing arithmetic. Open-coding
+ and - perfectly would have bought a few percent and added the one class
of defect the plan was written to avoid.

The cause, confirmed by a direct experiment rather than inferred from the
profile: LOOKUP-FUNCTION and FIND-DYNAMIC-BINDING walked the WHOLE
dynamic-frame chain before the namespace, so resolving a name cost
O(call-stack depth). The identical loop measured 2.7x slower forty frames
down. Fixed with an exact per-symbol count of live dynamic bindings; a
walk guaranteed to fail is skipped. Full account in
issues/closed/name-resolution-is-linear-in-stack-depth.issue.

    -O3 fib 27          619 -> 358 ms   1.73x
    -O3 loop, depth 1   236 -> 238 ms   1.00x   <- the control
    -O3 loop, depth 40  578 -> 232 ms   2.49x

The depth-1 row is the one worth keeping: UNCHANGED, because at depth 1
there was never anything to skip. That is what makes the other rows mean
what they claim. And the loop is now depth-invariant, which is the
property rather than any single number.

WHAT THIS SAYS ABOUT THE BACKLOG. The open-coding item was written from a
probe, not from a profile of this engine, and it was wrong about where
the time was -- not because the probe was bad but because it measured a
different thing. Re-profiled after the fix, the top is now the CALL
PROTOCOL:

    CALL-AUTOLISP-FUNCTION-IN-CONTEXT   20.7% self
    WRAP-BUILTIN-FUNCTION &rest lambda   7.4% self / 25.1% total
    LOOKUP-FUNCTION                      5.9% self
    all the arithmetic together         ~10%

So the next slice is the per-call cost -- the &rest argument list, the
call-stack cons, the HANDLER-CASE established around every builtin call --
and open-coding operators moves DOWN the list, to after that. Measure,
then act on the measurement; the plan is an input to that, not a
substitute for it.

Slice 13 (2.0.14) -- open-coding, guarded
-----------------------------------------

Slice 12 put the CALL PROTOCOL on top of the profile and open-coding
behind it. Measuring the protocol directly settled which to do first, and
it was not what either the profile or the plan said on its own:

    the protocol, in isolation      43 ns a call
      of which: the outer HANDLER-CASE   ~25%
                the call-stack cons      ~31%
                the second &rest list     ~9%

Trimming those pieces is worth 10-15% of the protocol, i.e. a few percent
overall, and the cleanest way to get most of it -- changing the subr
calling convention to take the argument LIST -- turned out to touch
MAKE-AUTOLISP-SUBR, which is exported and used by the CAD layer and by
eight test sites with an ordinary-function convention. A broad contract
change for a small measured win.

Open-coding, on the other hand, removes the WHOLE protocol for the
operators it covers -- not the ~10% the arithmetic itself costs. In fib,
four of the six calls per invocation are `<', `-', `+'. That is the
lever, and it is why the original plan named open-coding even though the
profile attributes little time to the builtins themselves: the profile
charges their CALLS to CALL-AUTOLISP-FUNCTION-IN-CONTEXT.

WHAT MAKES IT SAFE, since this is the item that was deliberately deferred
for years of slices on exactly this ground:

  It is a FAST PATH, not a second implementation. The inline code covers
  one narrow case -- two integers in the 32-bit range -- and everything
  else goes to the SAME builtin: floats, strings, nil, wrong arity, and
  out-of-range results. So every value, every error and every message
  outside that case is the builtin's, unchanged, because it IS the
  builtin.

  The narrowness is not caution for its own sake. AutoLISP `+' signals
  INTEGER-OVERFLOW outside the 32-bit range, so the fast path checks the
  RESULT and not merely the arguments; and `<' folds a NON-NUMERIC
  argument to nil rather than signalling, which loop guards depend on, so
  a float or a nil is handed over rather than "improved".

  The call site checks, per call, that the name still resolves to the
  exact builtin whose semantics were open-coded. `+' can be redefined
  with DEFUN, assigned with SETQ, or shadowed by a `/'-local; all three
  work, all three were checked against the running engine, and all three
  must reach the user's definition FROM COMPILED CODE. The subr carries a
  tag; a user definition is a different object with no tag; the guard
  fails and the ordinary call happens.

  The two tables -- *OPEN-CODED-CORE-BUILTINS* in the builtins and
  *OPEN-CODED-OPERATORS* in the transpiler -- are joined by that tag, so
  neither side can open-code something the other did not agree to. And
  the builtins side stamps the tag only after checking that the function
  behind the name really is the one named in the table, so swapping an
  implementation silently drops the tag: a missing tag costs a call, a
  wrong tag would cost correctness.

RESULT, measured by INTERLEAVING the two binaries in one run -- the
machine drifted enough between separate runs to invent a 12% regression
that was not there, and alternating them removes it:

                   before   after   speedup
    -O0 shallow     429      429     1.00x   <- control
    -O0 deep        380      384     0.99x   <- control
    -O3 fib 27      416      271     1.54x
    -O3 loop d1     248      177     1.40x
    -O3 loop d40    240      173     1.39x

The -O0 rows are the control and they are unchanged, as they must be:
open-coding only exists in compiled code. Work time alone (startup ~80 ms
removed) is about 1.75x.

TWO THINGS THIS SLICE LEARNED THE HARD WAY:

  Transpiler output must survive PRINT then READ. A .lap is produced by
  printing the transpiled code as source and COMPILE-FILEing it, and
  WRITE-LAP-SOURCE prints with *PRINT-CIRCLE* NIL -- so a GENSYM comes
  out as `#:FN364' at every occurrence and each one reads back as a
  DIFFERENT symbol. The binding and its references come apart and the
  artefact does not compile. That is why the existing %VALUE / %TEST /
  %COND are interned, and the new names are too. The artefact tests
  caught it immediately, which is the argument for having had them.

  A docstring closed early by an unescaped quote shipped in 2.0.13 and
  the whole corpus passed on both hosts. See
  issues/closed/docstring-closed-early-by-unescaped-quote.issue and the
  new `make check-docstrings'.

Slice 14 (2.0.15) -- the rest of the hot operators, and truth
-------------------------------------------------------------

Slice 13 built the mechanism and its guard for seven two-argument
arithmetic and relational operators. This extends it, which is what a
mechanism is for: each new operator costs only the exact statement of its
narrow case.

Re-profiling first, as usual. After slice 13 the profile is FLAT -- no
dominator left on fib -- but two things stood out and both are addressed
here:

    LOOKUP-FUNCTION                    10.2% self
    MAKE-DYNAMIC-BINDING                7.6% self
    NAMESPACE-BINDING-CELL              6.6% self
    CALL-AUTOLISP-FUNCTION-IN-CONTEXT   6.1% self  (was 20.7%)
    FIND-AUTOLISP-SYMBOL + STRING=*     ~4%

That last one is TRUTH. AutoLISP truth is the SYMBOL T, so every
predicate, every relational operator and every AND / OR has to produce
it -- and producing it hashed the name in the symbol table, every time.
It is now remembered in *AUTOLISP-TRUE-SYMBOL*, invalidated in the one
place the answer can change: RESET-AUTOLISP-SYMBOL-TABLE clears the
table, and clears this with it. Nothing else removes a symbol-table
entry; interning only ever adds. All 43 occurrences of
(intern-autolisp-symbol "T") went through it, so there is one spelling of
truth rather than a fast one and a slow one.

NEW OPERATORS: car cdr null not atom listp zerop eq 1+ 1- and unary -.
The mechanism generalised from `two arguments' to `the arities each fast
path is written for', because the SEMANTICS are per arity: (- 5) negates
and (- 9 4) subtracts, same builtin, two meanings, two fast paths.

The interesting ones are the ones that had to DECLINE:

  EQ is EQL, PLUS the rule that two strings with equal contents are EQ
  (the host interns literals). So the fast path declines whenever either
  argument is a string -- a plausible inline EQ would have quietly
  answered nil for (eq "abc" "abc"), which is the exact shape of the
  defect this whole design exists to prevent. Tested directly.

  CAR / CDR: nil -> nil, cons -> the part, anything else -> an error. CL
  CAR of NIL is NIL and LISTP is exactly `nil or cons', so the whole
  non-error domain is one line and a number still reaches the builtin and
  still signals.

  NULL / NOT / ATOM / LISTP are TOTAL -- they accept any object and cannot
  signal -- so their fast path has no type test at all. That makes the tag
  check the only thing between them and a user's redefinition, which is
  now its own test.

  ZEROP, unlike those, does signal on a non-number, so it keeps its guard;
  and floats go to the builtin.

RESULT, interleaved A/B against 2.0.14:

                   2.0.14   new    speedup
    -O3 lists        347     211    1.64x
    -O3 shallow      192     166    1.16x
    -O3 fib          265     259    1.02x   (nothing new applies)
    -O0 lists        599     563    1.06x   (the truth cache, interpreted)

A NOTE ON MEASURING. Best-of-five first reported fib 5% SLOWER, which
would have been a real puzzle since no new fast path applies to it. Ten
reps said 265 vs 259. Five reps is not enough on a machine doing other
work, and the honest response to a surprising number is another
measurement, not an explanation.

THE DOCSTRING CHECK EARNED ITS KEEP ON ITS FIRST OUTING. It caught a
BROKEN DOCSTRING IN THIS SLICE'S OWN CODE -- AUTOLISP-TRUE, where an
unescaped quote around the name T ended the string early, leaving a dead
form in the body. Written, shipped and caught the same afternoon; the
tests were all green with it in place, exactly as they were in 2.0.13.

Slice 15 (2.0.16) -- cheaper poll points
----------------------------------------

pjb's item 3, and slice 7's finding come round: poll points, not
interpretation, are what makes debugging slow. Measured before touching
anything (fib 25, in process):

    plain interpreted     184 ms
    plain compiled         80 ms
    DEBUGGED interpreted  324 ms    1.76x
    DEBUGGED compiled     168 ms    2.10x

And profiled (465 samples, debugged compiled fib 30) the debug-specific
work is about 42% of the run, of which CALL-WITH-POLL-POINT is 23% self.

THREE CHANGES, none of which cost anything in expressiveness:

  1. WHERE EXECUTION IS was a CONS, written on every poll point -- twice
     per instrumented form -- so a program merely being RUN under a
     session allocated two conses per form for a fact nobody outside the
     debugger reads. It is two fixnum slots now. The subtlety a boolean
     replaced: NIL used to mean `no poll point yet', which a cons can say
     and a pair of zeros cannot, since (0 . 0) is a real poll point --
     function 0's entry form. Hence the separate validity flag, and hence
     three tests for it.

  2. THE JUMP CHECKS. JUMP-DISPOSITION and JUMP-EXIT-CHECK each begin by
     reading the jump-target slot and doing nothing when it is NIL, which
     is the case on every poll point of every program that is not
     mid-jump. That was two full calls per instrumented form to learn
     nothing. The slot is read at the call site now. Same answers:
     JUMP-DISPOSITION cannot return :SKIP with no target, and
     JUMP-EXIT-CHECK is a no-op with none.

  3. THE STEP CHECK, the same shape. The Bloom summary and the watches
     list were already guarded by the slot that makes them possible; the
     step request was not.

Also CALL-WITH-COMPILED-POLL-POINT is now INLINE -- out of line it was a
whole call, per instrumented form, to read one special variable and
funcall through it.

RESULT. The wall clock on this machine has a noise floor around 10%,
which is more than the effect, so the primary measurement is ALLOCATION:
it is deterministic and exactly attributable.

                            2.0.15        2.0.16      change
    plain compiled          10,086,112   10,086,112    0        <- control
    DEBUGGED compiled       34,130,496   24,402,256   -28.5%
    DEBUGGED interpreted    82,027,104   71,950,656   -12.3%

The plain row is BYTE-IDENTICAL, which is the control: whatever else
happened, nothing outside the debugged path moved.

For time, 15 reps with the plain row carried as a drift control:

                            2.0.15   2.0.16
    plain compiled (control)   72       76      <- the run that got 76 was
    DEBUGGED compiled         176      152         on the BUSIER machine

The control moved AGAINST the new build and it still won. Debugging
overhead over plain: 104 ms -> 76 ms, i.e. down 27% -- which agrees with
the 28.5% allocation figure, and the agreement is the reason to believe
either.

WHAT WAS DELIBERATELY NOT DONE: the RESTART-CASE around every form is the
single biggest item left (about 5 ns per poll point, twice per form), and
swapping it for a CATCH is measured, small and local. It was left alone
because a restart is inspectable and a catch tag is not, and clautolisp
deliberately offers the host debugger through CLAL-COMMON-LISP -- so
removing a named restart from a DEBUGGER for a few nanoseconds is not a
trade to make silently. Written up with the measurement in
issues/open/poll-point-restart-cost.issue for pjb to settle, along with
the shadow-frame pool, which is blocked on what a SNAPSHOT is allowed to
keep.

Slice 16 (2.0.17) -- the calls that were not doing anything
-----------------------------------------------------------

Re-profiled the plain compiled path, as the backlog says to. It is flat
now -- no dominator -- and what the flatness revealed is that a large
share of it is CALL OVERHEAD FOR ONE-LINE FUNCTIONS:

    CALL-AUTOLISP-FUNCTION-IN-CONTEXT   12.0% self
    the compiled body itself             8.7%
    LOOKUP-FUNCTION                      7.1% self
    AUTOLISP-FUNCTION-TRACE-P            6.0%   <-
    EVALUATION-CONTEXT-DYNAMIC-FRAME     4.9%   <-
    SYMBOL-HAS-NO-DYNAMIC-BINDING-P      4.9%   <-
    CALLABLE-VALUE-P                     4.3%   <-
    NAMESPACE-BINDING-CELL               4.3%
    MAKE-DYNAMIC-BINDING                 3.8%
    FRAME-BINDING                        2.7% self  <-
    BINDING-CELL-BOUND-P                 2.7%   <-

The marked ones are all the same thing. EVALUATION-CONTEXT-DYNAMIC-FRAME
is, in full:

    (defun evaluation-context-dynamic-frame (context)
      (internal::evaluation-context-dynamic-frame context))

It exists so the internal slot reader has a public name. Out of line it
is a function call, on every variable read, every function resolution and
every call -- 4.9% of the profile to read one slot. BINDING-CELL-VALUE,
BINDING-CELL-BOUND-P and EVALUATION-CONTEXT-CURRENT-NAMESPACE are the
same shape; CALLABLE-VALUE-P, SYMBOL-HAS-NO-DYNAMIC-BINDING-P,
AUTOLISP-OPEN-CODE-TAG, AUTOLISP-TRUE-SYMBOL and AUTOLISP-FUNCTION-TRACE-P
are two-line predicates whose whole point is to answer `no' quickly.

Eleven DECLAIM INLINEs. Nothing else changed. It is the same lesson as
slice 15 found in the debugger -- a call to learn nothing is still a call
-- one layer down, and it is worth naming as a pattern rather than a
coincidence: a package boundary drawn by re-exporting accessors as
functions is free at the source level and is NOT free at run time.

RESULT, A/B/A with 15 reps each (fib 25, in process):

                    2.0.16   2.0.17   speedup
    interpreted      168      148      1.14x
    compiled          64       48      1.33x

The two runs of the new build gave 148 / 48 BOTH TIMES, identically, so
the window was quiet and the numbers are not drift. For scale: compiled
fib 25 was 80 ms when this session started looking at the debugger three
slices ago.

WHY INLINING IS SAFE HERE, since it is not always: an accessor IS the
slot, and none of these is a function anyone redefines or stubs -- checked
for SETF SYMBOL-FUNCTION / FDEFINITION over the whole tree, including the
tests. Callers that take #' of them still get the out-of-line definition,
which SBCL and CCL both still emit.

Slice 17 (2.0.18) -- a frame's bindings stop being an alist
-----------------------------------------------------------

The item slice 16 scoped and did not take. A frame held its bindings as an
alist of (symbol . binding): two conses per parameter and per `/'-local of
every call, spent recording a symbol THE BINDING ALREADY CARRIED in its
own SYMBOL slot. The bindings are now threaded together directly, by a
NEXT slot on the binding.

    per binding, before:  binding struct + 2 conses
    per binding, after:   binding struct (one slot wider)

Allocation, the deterministic measurement (fib 22, bytes consed):

                          2.0.17        2.0.18      change
    plain compiled       10,086,112    8,254,624    -18.2%
    DEBUGGED compiled    24,402,256   22,859,648     -6.3%
    DEBUGGED interpreted 71,950,656   70,063,200     -2.6%

Wall clock is unchanged within this benchmark's noise; the win is
allocation and the collector work behind it, which is what shows up in
the long-running loops real CAD code is made of rather than in fib.

WHY IT IS SOUND, and both halves were checked before writing a line:

  A binding belongs to EXACTLY ONE frame. The sole caller of (SETF
  FRAME-BINDING) is BIND-DYNAMIC-VARIABLE and it makes a fresh binding
  every time. A shared binding would need two NEXTs and would corrupt
  both chains.

  The representation is read in EXACTLY FOUR PLACES, all in api.lisp, and
  FRAME-BINDING is not exported -- the debugger reaches bindings through
  DYNAMIC-FRAME-BINDING-VALUE, which goes through it. The file already
  said so ("the only thing that should read this slot") and it was true.

ONE TRAP: the alist/hash-table discriminator was (LISTP BINDINGS). A chain
head is a STRUCT, not a list, so every one of those became HASH-TABLE-P.
Missing one would have read a binding as an alist -- silently, and only
for frames that had promoted.

TESTS. The PROMOTION path -- what a frame does once it holds more
bindings than a linear walk searches cheaply -- had NO TEST AT ALL before
this, and is not reachable from any ordinary program: it needs more than
a dozen locals in one function. It now has four, written against the
representation directly rather than through AutoLISP source, because the
representation is what changed: binding past the limit and resolving
every name across the promotion boundary; rebinding a name in one frame
(legal -- (defun f (a / a) ...)) replacing rather than adding, in BOTH
representations, since replacement is written twice; and nested frames
shadowing innermost-first with the live-binding counts following.

Verified load-bearing: disabling the chain's replace detection fails
REBINDING-A-SYMBOL-IN-ONE-FRAME-REPLACES-RATHER-THAN-ADDS and NOTHING
ELSE in the whole corpus -- which is the argument for having written it.

Slice 18 (2.0.19) -- the third batch of open-coded operators
------------------------------------------------------------

CONS, LIST, = and /=, plus the three-argument arities of + - * < > <= >=
and the one-argument arities of + and *. The mechanism and its guard are
from slice 13; this is the part that a mechanism makes cheap.

TWO THINGS NEW IN KIND:

  CONS and LIST are TOTAL. BUILTIN-CONS is (cons a b); BUILTIN-LIST
  returns its &rest list. Neither can signal and neither has a narrow
  case, so their fast path has NO GUARD beyond the tag check -- which
  makes the tag check the only thing between them and a user's
  redefinition, and gives that its own test.

  = and /= are NOT numeric-only, which is the trap the backlog note
  warned about. COMPARISON-EQUAL-P is EQL, then numeric = for two
  NUMBERS, then STRING= for two STRINGS. An inline `=' that knew only
  about numbers would answer nil for (= "a" "a") -- the same shape of
  defect as an inline EQ that forgot interned strings, and caught the
  same way: the fast path covers two 32-bit integers and declines
  everything else, including (= 1 1.0), where EQL says no and = says yes.

AND ONE THAT N-ARY INVITES: the relational chain is PAIRWISE ALONG the
arguments -- (< 1 3 2) is (and (< 1 3) (< 3 2)), which is NIL. Comparing
the first against each of the rest would say T. CL n-ary < agrees with
AutoLISP here, so the whole chain is one form, but the test says so out
loud because the wrong reading is the tempting one.

RESULT, A/B/A on a cons/list/=-heavy workload (building 900-element
lists 120 times, then scanning them):

                    2.0.18   2.0.19   speedup
    interpreted      212      208      1.00x   <- control
    compiled          76       36-40   ~2x

The interpreted row is the control and it is unchanged, as it must be:
open-coding exists only in compiled code. fib, which exercises none of
the new operators, is unchanged too (compiled 48 -> 44 ms, within noise)
-- worth checking because the arithmetic fast paths were rewritten to be
n-ary rather than two-argument.

Slice 19 (2.0.20) -- pjb settles the two poll-point questions
-------------------------------------------------------------

Slice 15 left two items measured but not taken, because both traded
something other than speed and neither was mine to trade. pjb settled both
(2026-08-30). Both premises were CHECKED before acting on them.

THE RESTART -> CATCH. His reading -- one restart, and a catch loses no
functionality -- is right on both counts: one RESTART-CASE establishes
CLAL-POLL-RETURN, two users find it BY NAME and invoke it, and there is NO
COMPUTE-RESTARTS anywhere in the product, which is what the
inspectability objection rested on. THROW reaches the innermost matching
CATCH, i.e. the innermost poll point, and `is one established?' is (PLUSP
POLL-DEPTH), already maintained around exactly that form. The :ABORT
resolutions beside it already unwound by THROW, so there is now one
mechanism instead of two.

THE SNAPSHOTS. His question -- are they only read for information, never
resumed from -- is the right one, and the answer made the change free:
BUILD-SNAPSHOT already converts every shadow frame into a fresh
STACK-FRAME eagerly, at the stopping point. A snapshot never retains a
DEBUG-FRAME. The copy he was willing to pay for was already there.

So slice 15's claim that pooling "would need the snapshot to copy what it
keeps, and that is a change to what a snapshot IS" was WRONG, and wrong
expensively: it deferred a change that was already safe. The blocker was
inferred from the shape of the problem instead of read out of the code --
which is the same mistake as assuming a test proves something about a
program, one topic over.

Spent frames now go to a free list, handed over IN THE VERY CONS they were
popped with, so a debugged call allocates neither a frame nor a cons once
the pool has warmed. The reference to the runtime dynamic-frame is cleared
on the way in, so a pooled frame does not pin a binding chain.

WHAT EACH BOUGHT, measured separately because they were decided
separately:

                        plain (control)  debugged   debugged alloc
    2.0.19                  48 ms         108 ms    22,859,648
    catch only              48 ms         104 ms    22,793,792
    catch + frame pool      48 ms         104 ms    20,082,256

The catch bought the TIME (~4% of the run, ~7% of the debugging overhead)
and no allocation -- SBCL's RESTART-CASE costs cycles, not heap. The pool
bought the ALLOCATION (-11.9%) and no further time. NEITHER BOUGHT BOTH,
and the micro-benchmark that motivated the swap (5.3 ns a poll point)
predicted more time than the real workload delivered: the usual gap
between a mechanism measured alone and the same mechanism in context.
Worth reporting as it came out rather than as it was pitched.

TESTS. `return' at a stop was already covered in three places -- the error
path, the normal-stop path (a regression test, from when
APPLY-RESUME-DIRECTIVE used to drop it) and the Emacs UI -- so the swap
had real cover from the start; disabling the throw fails the normal-stop
one with "7 is not EQL to 99". The pool got two new tests: a stop taken
AFTER twenty activations have filled and drained the pool still reports
the right functions on the stack (a reused frame keeping its old FID would
name the wrong one), and a pooled frame holds no dynamic-frame. The second
of those caught a mistake in its own first draft -- the pool is a list OF
FRAMES, the conses being the recycled links, and the test read it as a
list of conses.

Slice 20 (2.0.21) -- FOREACH and REPEAT stop falling back
---------------------------------------------------------

FIRST, A MEASUREMENT THAT KILLED A BACKLOG ITEM. Re-profiling the plain
compiled path shows WRAP-BUILTIN-FUNCTION gone from the top entirely:
open-coding removed the builtin calls it wrapped. So the CALL PROTOCOL
item -- the argument-list convention for the subr function slot, held
back because MAKE-AUTOLISP-SUBR is exported and a contract change --
is now MOOT for the workloads that motivated it. Removed from the
backlog rather than left to look actionable.

What the profile shows instead is the usubr call machinery
(BIND-USUBR-FRAME 34% cumulative) and function resolution (24%), both of
which are blocked or risky. So the useful work is no longer
micro-optimisation but COVERAGE.

THE HOLE. The transpiler handled QUOTE PROGN IF SETQ AND OR WHILE COND
and calls; everything else fell back. A fallback hands the interpreter
THE WHOLE SUBFORM -- body included -- so a compiled function containing a
FOREACH ran that entire loop interpreted. FOREACH and REPEAT are the two
commonest loops in AutoLISP, which made this by far the largest hole left
in what "compiled" meant.

How large is worth stating exactly, because a percentage would hide it:

                        2.0.20      2.0.21
    interpreted         1212 ms     1236 ms   <- control
    compiled            1208 ms      220 ms   5.5x

In 2.0.20 compiling that function bought NOTHING -- 1208 against 1212 --
because the loop was the workload and the loop was interpreted either
way. That row is the finding; the 5.5x is its consequence.

WHAT IS EMITTED IS THE SHAPE ONLY. Every decision -- the REPEAT count
check and its diagnostic, the FOREACH sequence check, and FOREACH's rule
for giving its variable a value -- was factored out of the interpreter
into CHECK-REPEAT-COUNT, CHECK-FOREACH-SEQUENCE and
BIND-FOREACH-VARIABLE, which BOTH now call. The alternative was restating
them in the transpiler, and a second statement of a rule is a second
thing that can be wrong. That rule matters here more than usual: FOREACH
ASSIGNS when the name is already bound anywhere in the chain, so a
FOREACH over a name an enclosing function binds writes THAT binding and
the value survives the loop. Quirk and all -- and it is in the corpus.

A BUG FOUND ON THE WAY, and it was already there. An empty loop body ---
`(while (setq i (cdr i)))', which is how AutoLISP drains a list --
compiled to (LOOP WHILE X DO NIL). LOOP's DO needs a compound form, so
SBCL built a function that signalled a COMMON LISP error when CALLED. It
worked interpreted. Nothing caught it because no corpus entry had an
empty loop body. issues/closed/compiled-loop-with-empty-body.issue.

pjb's fix, and better than the first one written: make TRANSPILE-BODY
return `(PROGN)' rather than NIL for an empty body. (PROGN) evaluates to
NIL so no value changes, but a transpiled body is now ALWAYS A COMPOUND
FORM and can go anywhere a form can. The first attempt wrapped the body
at each site that uses it as a statement, which works and leaves a rule
every future loop has to remember. His puts the invariant at the one
definition instead of in the head of whoever writes the next loop.

AND A MEASUREMENT THAT WAS MEASURING NOTHING. The first three loop
benchmarks reported 48 ms against 44 ms -- an unremarkable 8% -- and all
three were STILL RUNNING FIB: the benchmark had been derived from another
by string substitution, the anchor never matched, and the file kept its
old workload. The tell was that ten times the iterations produced
identical numbers. The rule that caught it is worth keeping: a benchmark
that does not respond to its own workload is not measuring it, and a
sanity line printing the program's own result costs nothing.

Slice 21 (2.0.23) -- LAMBDA and FUNCTION, and what measuring them found
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The two operators slice 20 named as worth having next. Both were
falling back, and a fallback hands the interpreter the WHOLE SUBFORM,
so the higher-order idiom portable AutoLISP is written in --
(apply (function f) args) -- put an AUTOLISP-EVAL back into the middle
of otherwise compiled code.

FUNCTION is QUOTE plus a hint to a compiler that is not this one, so a
well-formed (function X) is a CONSTANT and is now emitted as one. What
is folded is the DESIGNATOR, never a lookup: a symbol stays a symbol,
resolved against the dynamic chain when APPLY finally runs, which is
what lets (apply (function fn) args) find an FN defined further down
the stack. Folding the resolution would have been faster still and
wrong, and the test says so in a sentence rather than leaving it to be
rediscovered.

LAMBDA calls the interpreter's own EVAL-LAMBDA-FORM. The closure, the
environment capture, the arity check and the `/'-in-a-lambda-list
warning are all behaviour rather than mechanism, and this file restates
none of them; what is saved is the dispatch.

MEASURED, ISOLATED, with a control that must not move (speed 3):

    (function car) in a loop     12 ms  ->   4 ms
    control, (setq f 1)           4 ms  ->   4 ms

FUNCTION now costs exactly what a constant costs, because it IS one.
The control is unmoved, which is the only reason to believe the other
row.

MEASURED IN CONTEXT, and this is the honest half: in an (apply
(function car) (list p)) loop the same change is INVISIBLE -- 12 ms
either way -- because APPLY dominates and the fold removes something
APPLY's cost hides. The slice is a real win on the form and a wash on
that workload, and both numbers are reported because reporting only
the first would be true and misleading.

VERIFYING THE GATE. With the two branches disabled the coverage test
goes RED and the two VALUE tests stay GREEN -- correctly: the fallback
computes the same answers, which is the whole fallback design. So one
gate pins the optimization and two pin the contract, and it is worth
knowing which is which rather than claiming three.

FOUND WHILE MEASURING, and much worse than the slice is good: a LAMBDA
inside a hot loop, COMPILED, is 440x SLOWER than interpreted -- 26416 ms
against 60 ms, control flat. Compilation is decided per FUNCTION
OBJECT, but EVAL-LAMBDA-FORM mints a new object per iteration, so at
threshold 1 the host compiler runs once per iteration; at the shipped
default of 16 the same shape never compiles at all. Threshold 1 is what
the TEST SUITE sets. The A/B says it is older than this slice -- 24448 ms
with the branches disabled -- so slice 21 neither caused it nor cured
it; it is the first thing to have measured it. Written up with three
candidate fixes in issues/open/lambda-in-a-loop-never-compiles.issue.

STILL FALLING BACK after this slice: SET, LET, DEFUN, DEFUN-Q, TRACE,
UNTRACE, COMMAND, COMMAND-S, VLAX-FOR. The COMMAND family is dominated
by the host and not worth it; DEFUN inside a body is rare. LET and SET
are the next candidates on frequency.

Slice 22 (2.0.24) -- LET stops falling back, and takes its body with it
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

BricsCAD V26's undocumented CL-style LET, vendor-confirmed 2026-08-07
and registered as a special operator here. It was still falling back,
and the cost was not the binding: A FALLBACK HANDS THE INTERPRETER THE
WHOLE SUBFORM, so a LET took ITS ENTIRE BODY with it. A compiled
function whose work sat inside a LET ran that work interpreted -- the
same hole FOREACH was in slice 20, in a form nobody thinks of as a
loop.

MEASURED, with a control that must not move (200000 iterations):

    a loop INSIDE a LET   interpreted 64 ms   compiled 68 ms  ->  12 ms
    the same loop, no LET interpreted 64 ms   compiled 12 ms  ->  12 ms

THE 68-AGAINST-64 IS THE FINDING. Compiling that function gained
NOTHING before this slice -- it was very slightly slower than
interpreting it, the loop being the work and the loop being interpreted
either way. 5.7x is merely the consequence, and the unmoved control is
the only reason to believe it.

PARALLEL BINDING, the rule that dictates the emitted shape: every
init-form is evaluated in the ENCLOSING scope BEFORE any name is bound,
so (setq x 10) (let ((x 1) (y x)) (list x y)) is (1 10). The values are
therefore collected into a list first and the frame pushed only
afterwards. Binding while walking the list would have made it LET*
SILENTLY -- and BricsCAD V26 has no LET*, the same harvest having
proved it. The difference shows only when an init mentions a name the
same LET binds, which is exactly the case nobody writes a test for, so
there is now a test for it, asserted as a VALUE and not only as an
agreement.

THE DIALECT WARNING IS EMITTED AT RUN TIME, not folded at transpile
time. *AUTOLISP-DIALECT* is a variable and can change during a run, so
a warning decided while compiling would be the wrong warning for a
later call -- and would differ between the compiled and interpreted
paths, which is the one thing this compiler may not do.

Malformed binding lists go to the interpreter untouched:
PARSE-LET-BINDING is ASKED, in a handler, whether each binding parses,
rather than having its accepted shapes copied into the transpiler where
they could drift.

Same gate pattern as slice 21, and worth repeating because it is easy
to miscount: with the branch disabled the COVERAGE test goes RED and
the two SEMANTIC tests stay GREEN, correctly -- the fallback computes
the same answers. One gate pins the optimization, the others pin the
contract.

Slice 23 (2.0.25) -- the compilation decision moves from the object to the code
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The 440x pathology slice 21 found while measuring, fixed. It is worth
stating as a capability rather than as a speed-up, because the
capability is the bigger half: BEFORE THIS SLICE NO LAMBDA BODY WAS
EVER COMPILED at the shipped threshold, so every higher-order AutoLISP
program -- everything written with APPLY and a lambda -- was permanently
interpreted however hot it ran.

WHY. Compilation was decided per FUNCTION OBJECT, by CALL-COUNT. A
LAMBDA form evaluated in a loop mints a NEW object per iteration, so
the decision was retaken from scratch on objects that were all THE SAME
CODE: at the default threshold of 16 each closure reached a count of 1
and was discarded, and at threshold 1 -- which the TEST SUITE sets --
the host compiler ran once per iteration.

Each compiled LAMBDA form now has one USUBR-SITE, shared by every
closure it builds. MAYBE-COMPILE-USUBR counts on the site when there is
one and publishes the compiled body back to it, so the next closure
starts compiled rather than recompiling the same body.

MEASURED, control flat at 4 ms throughout:

    threshold      lam speed 3 before      after
        1              26416 ms            24 ms
       16 (shipped)    never compiled      28 ms   (56 ms interpreted)

Both regimes fall to the same change, which is what made fix (1) the
right one of the three the ticket listed: it corrects the QUESTION --
"is this CODE hot?" rather than "is this OBJECT hot?" -- instead of
working around the answer.

IDENTITY WITHOUT A WEAK TABLE. The obvious implementation, a hash table
keyed on the lambda's source form, needs WEAK KEYS so it does not hold
dead code alive -- and weak hash tables differ across SBCL, CCL and
CLISP, which is portability surface bought for nothing. The transpiler
instead emits (LOAD-TIME-VALUE (MAKE-USUBR-SITE) T): one site per
lambda form, made when the compiled code loads, however it got there --
including through a .lap, which is transpiled code printed as source
and compiled again, where a struct baked in as a literal would have had
to print and read back.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT SHARED: the ENVIRONMENT (two closures from one
form may legitimately capture different ones -- that was the rejected
fix (3), and a test would fail if it ever started travelling with the
body); the INSTRUMENTED compiled body (its form-ids are tied to one
function's debug metadata, so sharing it would step the debugger
against the wrong record); and :FAILED (kept on the closure, so a body
that will not compile is retried on the next closure -- rare enough to
leave alone, and cheaper than a third state).

INTERPRETED CODE IS UNCHANGED, site NIL, decision on the closure. A
lambda in an INTERPRETED loop therefore still mints undecidable
closures. Recorded rather than hidden: it costs nothing today because
the enclosing loop is interpreted anyway, and closing it would need
exactly the source-form identity, and the weak table, that the compiled
path was able to avoid.

Slice 24 (2.0.26) -- VLAX-FOR, and the loop that was left behind
-----------------------------------------------------------------

THE LAST FORM THAT TOOK A BODY WITH IT when it fell back, and the one
where that hurt most in practice: Visual LISP automation is written as
a VLAX-FOR over a collection with all the work inside, so a compiled
function doing COM work ran every bit of it interpreted. Open-coded to
the same shape as FOREACH, with the members coming from the
interpreter's own path -- the runtime cannot reach the host COM
protocol, and the compiler certainly cannot, so both ask
VLAX-COLLECTION-ITEMS, which consults the hook the builtins layer
installs.

The COM-absent check is a SEPARATE function from the members lookup,
and called BEFORE the collection form is evaluated, because that is the
interpreter's order: with COM absent, (vlax-for x (some-form-with-a-
side-effect) ...) signals WITHOUT running the side effect. A compiler
that evaluated first and complained second would differ in exactly the
way nothing notices until it matters. There is a test.

THE BUG FOUND WHILE DOING IT. VLAX-FOR still bound its loop variable
with ASSIGN-IF-ALREADY-BOUND -- the very code FOREACH carried until
AutoCAD was probed in 2.0.22 and answered that it BINDS. The rule was
extracted into BIND-FOREACH-VARIABLE precisely so it would live in one
place, and VLAX-FOR was not brought along. So the two loops in this
implementation DISAGREED about their own loop variable: a VLAX-FOR over
a name a caller had bound wrote THAT binding and left the last member
of the collection in it. Both now call BIND-FOREACH-VARIABLE.

Asserted BY ANALOGY, not by a probe: no vendor evidence covers
VLAX-FOR, whose spec entry says only "Iterates over an ActiveX
collection object" and is SILENT about the loop variable. What the
tests pin is the AGREEMENT between the two loops.
issues/open/vlax-for-binding-rule-unprobed.issue says what would settle
it and why it has not been written -- it needs a live COM collection,
which the headless probe suite cannot conjure.

AND THE TEST THAT COULD NOT GO RED, which is the part worth carrying
forward. The first version of the binding test asserted the rule with
the suite's own settings, which compile a body eagerly -- so it
exercised only the TRANSPILED branch, which was already correct, and
stayed GREEN with the interpreter's old code put back. The second
version forced both paths and STILL stayed green, because it used a
GLOBAL: assign-if-bound and bind AGREE on a global, since no dynamic
binding is found and the old code bound too. Only a `/'-LOCAL OF THE
CALLER separates them -- which is exactly the case the AutoCAD probe
used, and which I had in front of me. The test now runs both paths over
a caller's local and goes red when the bug is put back. Verified, not
assumed: this is the third time in this compiler's history that a green
suite has meant nothing (2.0.22's foreach fix passed 31 suites before
AND after), and the pattern is always the same -- the test exercised a
case where the two behaviours coincide.

Slice 25 (2.0.27) -- SET, and taking the evaluation order from the host
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

The last FREQUENT fallback. SET is SETQ with the PLACE FORM EVALUATED,
which is the whole difference and the reason it cannot be folded into a
SETQ however constant the place looks: the name being assigned is not
known until run time.

Worth a small note on what was NOT restated. The transpiler emits the
place and the value as ARGUMENTS to SET-AUTOLISP-PLACE, so Common
Lisp's left-to-right argument evaluation gives the interpreter's LET*
order for free -- place first, then value -- and the symbol check
happens after both, inside that function, exactly where the interpreter
does it. Taking the order from the host rather than writing it out is
the same move as asking the runtime what a special operator is: fewer
places to be wrong.

That order has its own test, and it is a REAL gate rather than a
restatement: K ends at 11 when the place runs first and at 1 when the
value does, so the two orders differ in the ANSWER and not merely in
the path. Verified by emitting the value first on purpose -- the test
goes red, and the coverage test goes red with the branch disabled.

WHAT REMAINS FALLING BACK, and the list is now stable: DEFUN, DEFUN-Q,
TRACE, UNTRACE, COMMAND, COMMAND-S. None takes a body, so none of them
un-compiles work the way FOREACH, LET and VLAX-FOR did; each costs a
dispatch and nothing more. The COMMAND family is dominated by the host,
DEFUN inside a body is rare, and TRACE/UNTRACE are debugging verbs
whose cost is not in the dispatch. THE TRANSPILER-COVERAGE THREAD THAT
SLICE 20 OPENED IS THEREFORE FINISHED unless one of those turns out to
matter; what is left in this file is the allocation and configuration
work, not coverage.

Slice 26 (2.0.28) -- the compiler says when it is asked absurdly often
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The last piece of lambda-in-a-loop-never-compiles.issue, and the reason
it was worth finishing rather than closing with the fix: THE COMPILER
HAD NO IDEA HOW OFTEN IT WAS BEING ASKED. A lambda in a loop invited it
to run once per iteration -- 60000 times, 26 seconds absorbed in
silence -- and the shape was found by BENCHMARKING, which is not a way
to find bugs.

COUNT-USUBR-COMPILATION now counts both compilation paths, the
threshold one and the eager one, and warns ONCE past
*USUBR-COMPILATION-WARNING-THRESHOLD*. Nothing is refused: the warning
says the same body is probably being compiled repeatedly, and that this
is a bug in what DECIDES to compile rather than in the code being
compiled -- which is exactly what was true, and exactly what nobody was
told.

ONCE, not every time: a diagnostic that fires 59000 times is the same
silence with more output.

THE THRESHOLD IS ABSURD RATHER THAN TIGHT, and that is now MEASURED
instead of asserted. The whole compiler test suite -- every equivalence
case, every open-coding case, the instrumented and whole-corpus runs --
compiles 56 bodies. The threshold is 1000: an eighteenfold margin, and
the shape it exists to catch overshot it by sixty. A tight threshold
would have to be RIGHT; this one only has to be unreachable by honest
work, and now there is a number saying it is.

STILL OPEN after slice 26
-------------------------
  - a host-independent .lap. Deferred on purpose (see the closed
    clal-compiled-artefact.issue); CLISP is the existence proof that it
    can be done and the reason it costs speed.
  - poll points: slice 15 took 27% off the overhead and slice 19 took the
    last two measured items (issues/closed/poll-point-restart-cost.issue).
    What remains there is the per-poll-point work itself -- two
    POLL-POINT calls, the enter/exit bookkeeping and the UNWIND-PROTECT --
    none of which is removable without giving up something the debugger
    does.
  - TRANSPILER COVERAGE is CLOSED as a thread (slices 20-25). NO FORM
    THAT TAKES A BODY FALLS BACK ANY MORE -- that was the property
    worth chasing, since a fallback hands over the whole subform and
    so silently un-compiles the work inside it. Still falling back, and
    deliberately: DEFUN, DEFUN-Q, TRACE, UNTRACE, COMMAND, COMMAND-S.
    None takes a body; each costs a dispatch and nothing more. Reopen
    only with a measurement showing one of them matters.
  - (done in slice 26: the compilation counter and its one-shot
    warning, which was the last part of
    issues/closed/lambda-in-a-loop-never-compiles.issue.)
  - a lambda in an INTERPRETED loop still mints closures no decision
    can be taken about: the site covers the compiled path only. Costs
    nothing today, since the enclosing loop is interpreted anyway.
  - open-coding has reached diminishing returns for the OPERATORS, but
    not for the shapes. What was deliberately NOT taken in slice 18:
    LENGTH, NTH, MEMBER and ASSOC each call REQUIRE-PROPER-LIST, which
    walks the list, before doing their own walk -- so the call overhead
    a fast path would remove is small beside the work, and a fast path
    that walked once would be a second implementation with different
    behaviour on a dotted list. They are the wrong shape for this
    mechanism, which is worth recording so nobody adds them on the
    grounds that they are frequent.
  - what a call still allocates, after slice 17 removed the alist conses:
    a dynamic-frame struct and a dynamic-binding struct per parameter,
    plus the &rest list and the three-cons call-stack entry in
    CALL-AUTOLISP-FUNCTION-IN-CONTEXT. The frame and binding structs
    cannot simply be pooled -- a closure captures its environment and the
    debugger's snapshot reaches frames -- so that one needs the ownership
    rules restated first, exactly like the shadow-frame pool in
    poll-point-restart-cost.issue.
  - reading the qualities from the CONFIGURATION MODULE, so a project
    can set them once rather than on every invocation. The --optimize
    half is done (slice 11); this is the other half of the same item,
    and belongs with the deferred "gather options / getenv / defaults
    into the configuration module" work.
